{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And they rose early on the morrow, etc.</b> It is difficult: why did HaShem, blessed be He, not make it felt to Moses at the time of the act of the calf, immediately and instantly, to say: Go down, for your people has corrupted itself — only after the day of the act had passed, and on the morrow, after they had brought burnt-offerings and peace-offerings? Furthermore, if He had taken umbrage at the matter immediately and instantly to say to Moses: Go down, etc., they would not have come to slaughter to other elohim. And one cannot say that He had first said it to him but Moses delayed in order to placate the wrath, for in the words of HaShem it is stated: ״וַיִּשְׁתַּחֲווּ לוֹ וַיִּזְבְּחוּ לוֹ״ — from this you learn that His words were after they had slaughtered, on the morrow of the act. And although the Sages of blessed memory said (Avodah Zarah 4b) that Israel were not worthy of that act, etc., but rather if a congregation sins, etc. — even so, this must be learned even if they had not slaughtered, for by the first statement they made — ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — they had already accepted it upon themselves as an elohah. And although we have said that some of them said so and not all of them, and according to those who say it was the mixed multitude who said it — in any event they all heard and were all silent. And perforce one must say that it was the minority who did not agree, for if it had been the majority, they would have stood against them and killed those who said so, and if so the majority are liable by the law of a guarantor." "<b>And it appears</b> to say, that inasmuch as HaShem had previously told Moses that there he would dwell forty days, and after the forty He would give him the tablets — as they said of blessed memory (Shabbat 89a) on the verse ״כִּי בֹשֵׁשׁ מֹשֶׁה״ that [the people] came six hours into the day at the boundary of the forty — from this you learn that it had been told to him by HaShem that there he would dwell forty; therefore HaShem did not tell Moses to descend from the mountain before the forty days until their conclusion, and only then did He say to him: ״Go down from the mountain,״ and His saying ״Go down״ (רֵד) I will explain in its place. And according to this, although HaShem made felt the evil act of Israel from the time they assembled upon Aaron, with all this the word of our Elohim that He said forty days stands forever." "And if you say: why did HaShem not devise plans so that they would not stumble — this is a general inquiry into the conduct of the Creator with His creatures, and we have already written in parashat Bereshit (Genesis 6:5) some of the reasons that are graspable to us. But regarding what is before us, we need no reasons, for its reason is at its side, for perforce Moses had to be there forty days to receive the tablets, and there are hidden reasons of HaShem and reasons revealed to us — parallel to the formation of a child, and also to make known the gradations of the Torah above the entire world, for the entire world was created in six days, while the tablets — their creation was forty days. As Rabbi Yochanan said to Rabbi Chiya (Exodus Rabbah 47:5) regarding the fact that he [Moses] purchased fields, vineyards, and olives — for he sold a thing given to six [days] and acquired a thing given to forty [days]." "Yet another reason appears to me, for inasmuch as the Torah contains four general paths, and they are the count of PaRDeS, you should know that they are from four gradations of supernal lights called by the names Atzilut, Beriah, Yetzirah, Asiyah. The peshat — its light shines in the world of Asiyah; remez in the world of Yetzirah; derash in the world of Beriah; sod in the world of Atzilut. And we have already prefaced many times that there is no gradation in holiness whose height is not tenfold, and so before you are forty. Therefore he was there forty complete days, and if even one day had been missing, the Torah would have been lacking that corresponding aspect, and the ways of HaShem are just. And there are yet many other reasons, and with this there is no difficulty before our Elohim. And see the verse ״לֶךְ רֵד״ which follows this, in another manner."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The Talmudic citation (עבודה זרה ד,ב) follows the Vilna Shas page-numbering convention (ד,ב = 4b). The phrase אֵלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים is rendered 'other elohim' to preserve the divine-name transliteration rule.", 2 "The midrashic parable (Exodus Rabbah 47:5) alludes to a tradition that Moses relinquished the natural world (created in six days) in order to receive the Torah (given over forty days); the elliptical reference is rendered literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:53.443986542Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}